===============================================================================
= 3.1. Client command MAP                                                     =
===============================================================================
 This server-to-client command updates the visible isometric map. The framed,
 multi-level form described here is the only MAP format beginning with socket
 protocol version 1068. A server MUST reject older clients before sending it,
 and a client MUST reject an older server before requesting game state. All
 multi-byte integers use the network byte order defined in section 1.1.

 The packet begins with update_type (uint8):

  - 0 (SAME): the player remains on the same physical map. No map metadata
    follows.
  - 1 (NEW): the client MUST discard its previous physical-map state.
  - 2 (CONNECTED): the player crossed one of the previous map's tiled links;
    compatible cached levels are shifted instead of all being discarded.

 NEW and CONNECTED are followed by this common metadata, in order:

  - map_name (string)
  - background_music (string)
  - weather (string)
  - height_diff (uint8): zero or one
  - region_has_map (uint8): zero or one
  - region_name (string)
  - region_long_name (string)
  - map_path (string)

 For NEW, the metadata is followed by physical_map_width (uint8) and
 physical_map_height (uint8). Both MUST be nonzero. For CONNECTED, it is
 instead followed by:

  - tile (uint8): 1 through 8 for a horizontal tiled link, 9 for TILED_UP, or
    10 for TILED_DOWN;
  - map_x_offset (sint8);
  - map_y_offset (sint8); and
  - depth_offset (sint8): zero for tile 1 through 8, +1 for tile 9, and -1 for
    tile 10.

 Every update type then carries player_x (uint8), player_y (uint8),
 player_sub_layer (uint8 in the range 0 through 6), and level_count (uint8 in
 the range 1 through 13). A NEW player's coordinates MUST be inside the stated
 physical dimensions.

 Exactly level_count framed level blocks follow. Each block contains:

  - depth (sint8): relative to the player's physical map, from -6 through +6;
    zero is the player's map, negative values follow TILED_DOWN, and positive
    values follow TILED_UP;
  - payload_size (uint32): the exact number of bytes in this level's tile-record
    stream; and
  - payload (payload_size bytes).

 Depths MUST be unique and depth zero MUST be present. No bytes may occur after
 the final framed block. The client maintains one delta cache per depth in the
 current update. A depth omitted from the new level set becomes inactive and
 its cache may be freed. On CONNECTED, caches are shifted by depth_offset before
 the received deltas are applied.

 Each level payload contains zero or more tile records. Each record begins with
 mask (uint16). Bits 15 through 11 are the client-local X coordinate and bits
 10 through 6 are client-local Y. Both coordinates MUST be inside the negotiated
 wire look size. Bits 5 through 0 have these meanings:

  - 0x01 (SUPPORT_HEIGHT): structural_support_height (sint16) follows.
  - 0x02 (CLEAR): mark the complete cell as fog. No other fields follow this
    tile record. CLEAR may be combined only with HARD_CLEAR.
  - 0x04 (LIGHT_LEVEL): one light_level (uint8) for sub-layer zero follows.
  - 0x08 (LIGHT_LEVEL_MORE): six light_level values (uint8), ordered by
    sub-layer one through six, follow.
  - 0x10 (HARD_CLEAR): valid only with CLEAR; discard cached fog geometry rather
    than retaining its structural silhouette.
  - 0x20 (FOW): fow (uint8, zero or one) follows.

 Non-CLEAR fields occur in the order listed above, followed by num_layers
 (uint8, 0 through 49) and exactly that many layer records. LIGHT_LEVEL and
 LIGHT_LEVEL_MORE are independent. A light_level of zero means completely
 unlit and 255 means fully lit. Intermediate values are perceptually normalized
 scalar illumination and clients SHOULD interpolate them spatially. Omitted
 light fields retain their previous cached value.

 Each layer record begins with layer_type (uint8). Values 0 through 48 identify
 one real layer: seven object layers for each of seven sub-layers. Layer IDs in
 one tile record MUST be unique. A layer_type of 255 (LAYER_CLEAR) is followed
 only by actual_layer (uint8, 0 through 48), which clears that layer. Other
 layer records continue with face (uint16), object_flags (uint8), and
 layer_flags (uint8). layer_flags contains:

  - 0x01 (MULTI): quick_position (uint8) follows.
  - 0x02 (NAME): name (string), then name_color (string), follow.
  - 0x04 (ANIMATION): speed (uint8), facing (uint8), and animation_flags
    (uint8) follow. If animation_flags has MOVING (0x01), state (uint8) also
    follows. The remaining defined animation flags are ATTACKING (0x02),
    STOP_MOVING (0x04), and STOP_ATTACKING (0x08).
  - 0x08 (HEIGHT): height (sint16) follows.
  - 0x10 (INFRAVISION): no field; render this object highlighted.
  - 0x20 (ALIGN): horizontal_align (sint16) follows.
  - 0x40 (DOUBLE): no field; draw the face twice vertically.
  - 0x80 (MORE): extended_flags (uint32) follows.

 Optional extended fields follow extended_flags in this order:

  - 0x001 (ALPHA): alpha (uint8).
  - 0x002 (ROTATE): rotation_degrees (sint16).
  - 0x004 (ZOOM): zoom_x (uint16), then zoom_y (uint16).
  - 0x008 (TARGET): object_id (uint32), then is_friend (uint8, zero or one).
  - 0x010 (PROBE): hit_point_percentage (uint8, 0 through 100).
  - 0x020 (PRIORITY): no field; draw in the priority pass.
  - 0x040 (SECOND_PASS): no field; draw in the second alpha pass.
  - 0x080 (GLOW): glow_color (string), then glow_speed (uint8).
  - 0x100 (ROOF): no field; this wall-layer object is a camera/roof surface.
  - 0x200 (DOOR): no field; this object is a door eligible for a local camera
    hint.

 After all layer records, tile_extended_flags (uint8) follows. Its only defined
 bit is 0x01 (ANIMATION). If set, anim_count (uint8, 0 through 7) follows, then
 anim_count records of sub_layer (uint8, 0 through 6), animation_type (uint8;
 1 DAMAGE or 2 KILL), and value (sint16). Animation sub-layers in one tile
 record MUST be unique.

 The server remains authoritative for line of sight, fog, and whether an object
 is included. Smooth interpolation is cosmetic and MUST NOT reveal omitted
 objects. A client MUST validate the entire MAP packet, including every framed
 payload and string terminator, before applying metadata, scrolling caches, or
 changing any cell. Unknown flags, invalid indices, duplicate depths/layers,
 inconsistent connected depth offsets, truncation, size mismatches, or trailing
 bytes invalidate the complete command; the client MUST log and ignore it
 without partially applying an earlier block.

===============================================================================
= 3.4 and 3.7. Shared ITEM flag semantics                                     =
===============================================================================
 The ITEM command's item records and the ITEM_UPDATE command when its
 UPD_FLAGS bit is set contain a flags field encoded as uint32. The following
 bits are defined:

  - 1: applied
  - 2: unpaid
  - 4: identified magical item
  - 8: cursed
  - 16: damned
  - 32: open container
  - 64: inventory locked
  - 128: trapped
  - 256: two-handed weapon
  - 512: lore book has already granted its one-time reading experience

 Unknown bits MUST be retained on the decoded item when practical and MUST NOT
 make the packet invalid. After a lore book grants reading experience, the
 server MUST send ITEM_UPDATE with UPD_FLAGS so clients can immediately stop
 treating that physical book as unread. The bit is persistent object state;
 item UIDs are connection-local and MUST NOT be used as durable read identity.

===============================================================================
= 3.19. Client command CHARACTERS                                             =
===============================================================================
 This command reports account metadata and the account's available characters
 after a successful account login or registration.

 An empty payload indicates that an account operation failed and returns the
 client to its account-login state.

 The packet MUST be constructed in the following order:
  - account (string):
     The authenticated account name.
  - connection_id (string):
     The current connection's diagnostic ID. This is exactly 32 lowercase
     hexadecimal characters. For QUIC, both endpoints derive the same opaque
     value from the completed TLS handshake. It is stable across QUIC path
     migration and MUST NOT be treated as an address or authentication token.
  - previous_connection_id (string):
     The diagnostic ID stored after the account's previous successful login,
     or an empty string when no previous ID is available.
  - previous_login (uint64):
     UTC timestamp of the account's previous successful login.

 The remainder contains zero or more character records. Each record has this
 field order:
  - archetype (string)
  - name (string)
  - region_name (string)
  - animation_id (uint16)
  - level (uint8)

 A client MUST reject a packet whose fixed fields or any character record are
 truncated. A connection ID is diagnostic metadata only and MUST NOT be used
 for authorization or banning.

===============================================================================
= 3.28. Client command KEEPALIVE                                               =
===============================================================================
 This command is the server-to-client keepalive response. It has no payload.

===============================================================================
= 3.29. Client command ASSET                                                   =
===============================================================================
 This command returns an asset response over the established QUIC game
 connection. The packet begins with these fields:

  - status (uint8):
     0 (OK), 1 (NOT_FOUND), 2 (NOT_MODIFIED), 3 (METADATA), or
     4 (METADATA_NOT_FOUND).
  - path (string):
     The path from the corresponding request.

 NOT_FOUND, NOT_MODIFIED, and METADATA_NOT_FOUND end after path. NOT_MODIFIED
 is valid only when the client supplied a cached size and SHA-256 digest that
 match the current asset.

 A METADATA response continues with total_size (uint32) and digest (32 bytes).
 These values come from the server's immutable startup asset snapshot and are
 authenticated by the established, certificate-pinned QUIC connection. A
 client using a separate HTTP origin MUST obtain this metadata first and MUST
 reject an HTTP body whose exact size or SHA-256 digest differs.

 An OK response continues with these fields:

  - total_size (uint32):
     Complete asset size.
  - offset (uint32):
     Offset of this chunk.
  - digest (32 bytes):
     SHA-256 digest of the complete asset. Every OK chunk carries the same
     digest, allowing the client to reject a response that changes mid-stream.
  - data (bytes):
     Up to 60000 asset bytes.

 The client MUST request continuation chunks until total_size bytes have been
 received. Assets larger than 128 MiB MUST be rejected. Before making the data
 available or replacing its persistent cache, the client MUST calculate the
 complete SHA-256 digest and reject a mismatch. Cache replacement MUST use a
 same-directory temporary file with exclusive creation followed by an atomic
 rename. Cached assets MUST be scoped to the stable server ID or authenticated
 certificate fingerprint. A client MAY use a digest of the endpoint only for
 a manual entry that has no directory server identity.

